"The letter 'waw' at the beginning of the surah is a particle of oath (qasam). 'Adiyat' means those that run swiftly; they can be horses, camels, or warriors. 'Dabh' is the panting sound of breath while running. 'Qadh' means striking sparks repeatedly, that is, striking sparks from stones with hooves. This indicates that the horses run swiftly at night. Even though some commentators interpreted this as tongues that kindle the fire of war or deceitful schemes, its most evident meaning is horses carrying out a night raid. Modern motorized vehicles also fall within this scope.\n\n'Mughirat' means those that raid in the morning. 'Naq'an' means raising clouds of dust. This verbal clause, being coordinated with the preceding participles, yields the meaning: 'horses running breathlessly, striking fire from their hooves, launching a morning raid, raising clouds of dust.' The most widely accepted commentary on these verses is that they are the horses of cavalrymen. However, there are also those who state they are the camels of the pilgrims. In addition, it has been narrated that 'qadh' refers to enmity with the tongue, and 'mughirat' refers to bayonets.\n\nAs the response to the oath: 'Indeed, mankind is truly ungrateful to his Lord.' 'Kanud' means one who denies blessings, a miser, and a faultfinder. In a hadith, it is described as: 'He eats alone, beats his slave, and withholds his due.' Man is himself a witness to this ingratitude; he either knows it in his conscience or will confess it in the Hereafter.\n\nAnd man is exceedingly stubborn due to his intense love of worldly wealth. 'Khayr' ('khayr') here means wealth. 'Shadid' means stingy or strong. That is, man loves wealth presuming it to be good, acts stingily, or is greedy in acquiring it. Yet in the Hereafter, when that which is inside the graves is overturned and that which is in the breasts is brought forth, everything will be exposed and man will understand what he has done.\n\nOn that Day, their Lord is fully aware of everything concerning them. The wealth hoarded in this world will be heated in the fire of Hell, and their owners' foreheads, flanks, and backs will be branded with it. This is stated in Surah at-Tawbah, verse 35. Its worldly example is the war raids described at the beginning of the surah. However, the punishment of the Hereafter is eternal. As al-Razi said: 'O man, while you make preparations for worldly benefit, what are you preparing for your Lord?' It is with this warning that people are encouraged to spend in the way of Allah."